1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:05,033
What if you woke up tomorrow, and every price tag in the world showed a new number?

2
00:00:05,353 --> 00:00:09,591
Not dollars. Not euros. Hours. Hours of your life it takes to pay for that thing.

3
00:00:09,911 --> 00:00:17,307
A coffee: nine minutes. A new phone: almost thirty two hours. A new car: over a thousand hours of your life.

4
00:00:17,627 --> 00:00:22,688
Today you get that superpower. And by the end, you'll never look at a shopping cart the same way.

5
00:00:23,488 --> 00:00:26,716
First, how many hours is a dollar? We need your hourly pay.

6
00:00:27,036 --> 00:00:35,372
In October twenty twenty six, the average private sector worker in the US earned about thirty eight dollars an hour, before tax.

7
00:00:35,692 --> 00:00:43,297
Your number might be higher or lower. And after tax, it's lower for almost everyone. But let's use thirty eight to keep it simple.

8
00:00:43,617 --> 00:00:49,665
So every thirty eight dollars costs you one hour of your life. That's the exchange rate of your new superpower.

9
00:00:49,985 --> 00:00:58,844
In Europe the numbers are different, but the idea is exactly the same. Take your pay after tax, divide by your hours, and that's your price of one hour.

10
00:00:59,644 --> 00:01:01,432
Monday morning. The coffee shop.

11
00:01:01,752 --> 00:01:07,417
The barista smiles. A large oat latte with caramel? Your eyes see the tag: nine minutes.

12
00:01:07,737 --> 00:01:11,232
Nine minutes doesn't sound like much. But you buy one every workday.

13
00:01:11,552 --> 00:01:17,194
That's about three and a half hours a month. Almost half a working day, every month, for coffee.

14
00:01:17,514 --> 00:01:20,080
You still buy it. But now you actually taste it.

15
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:25,834
Because something you pay for with your life should at least be enjoyed, not swallowed on the way to the bus.

16
00:01:26,634 --> 00:01:32,218
Tuesday. The supermarket. This is where the superpower gets loud.

17
00:01:32,538 --> 00:01:37,948
Every snack shouts its hours at you. The fancy brand cereal: eighteen minutes. The store brand next to it: ten.

18
00:01:38,268 --> 00:01:45,525
Pre-cut fruit, bottled water, ready meals. Each one only a few minutes. But a weekly shop is made of fifty little decisions.

19
00:01:45,845 --> 00:01:51,278
You don't buy the cheapest thing every time. You just stop paying for convenience you don't even want.

20
00:01:51,598 --> 00:01:57,589
At the checkout, the total is about an hour less than last week. Nobody else even notices. You do.

21
00:01:58,389 --> 00:02:02,127
At lunch, Max shows you pictures of a new car. Forty thousand dollars. He's already picked the colour.

22
00:02:02,447 --> 00:02:08,345
Above the car, your superpower shows the real price. One thousand and fifty three hours.

23
00:02:08,665 --> 00:02:14,818
That's about twenty six full work weeks. Half a year of Mondays, just for the car. Before insurance, fuel or repairs.

24
00:02:15,138 --> 00:02:19,968
Max looks at the number for a long time. Then he asks if the red one is more expensive.

25
00:02:20,768 --> 00:02:22,080
Wednesday, the superpower shows you something sneakier. Your hourly pay is probably lower than you think.

26
00:02:22,400 --> 00:02:29,679
Say you work forty hours a week, but you also spend an hour every day getting to and from work. Unpaid.

27
00:02:29,999 --> 00:02:36,071
That's forty five hours of your life for forty hours of pay. Your thirty eight dollars an hour quietly becomes about thirty four.

28
00:02:36,391 --> 00:02:42,719
Add work clothes, lunches out and the after work takeaway because you're too tired to cook, and it drops even more.

29
00:02:43,039 --> 00:02:48,356
So when you translate prices into hours, use your real hours, not the ones on the contract.

30
00:02:49,156 --> 00:02:52,105
Evening. You open your phone and the superpower lights up your subscriptions.

31
00:02:52,425 --> 00:02:56,756
Streaming, music, cloud storage, an app you forgot, a gym you visit twice a year.

32
00:02:57,076 --> 00:03:01,673
Ninety dollars a month in this example. That's two hours and twenty minutes a month. Over twenty eight hours a year.

33
00:03:01,993 --> 00:03:07,914
Three and a half work days a year, paid automatically, for things you barely notice.

34
00:03:08,234 --> 00:03:11,450
You cancel two of them before bed. That's a few hours of your year, back.

35
00:03:12,250 --> 00:03:16,581
And then the big one. Rent.

36
00:03:16,901 --> 00:03:23,878
In this example, eighteen hundred dollars a month. Forty seven hours. More than a full work week, every month, just for the roof.

37
00:03:24,198 --> 00:03:29,934
You can't stop paying rent. But now you understand why housing is the biggest decision in most people's money.

38
00:03:30,254 --> 00:03:37,638
A slightly cheaper place, or a roommate, can be worth more hours than a lifetime of skipped coffees.

39
00:03:38,438 --> 00:03:43,349
Then your phone does the most dangerous thing a phone can do. It shows you an ad for a newer phone.

40
00:03:43,669 --> 00:03:49,787
Eleven hundred and ninety nine dollars in this example. Thirty one and a half hours.

41
00:03:50,107 --> 00:03:59,221
And here's the trick. If you upgrade every two years instead of every four, you pay those thirty one hours twice as often.

42
00:03:59,541 --> 00:04:03,767
Over twenty years, that's the difference between about one hundred and sixty hours and over three hundred. Four extra work weeks, for a slightly better camera.

43
00:04:04,087 --> 00:04:06,839
Your current phone suddenly looks fantastic.

44
00:04:07,159 --> 00:04:12,232
You put it in a new case, clean the screen, and it feels almost new. Zero hours spent.

45
00:04:13,032 --> 00:04:21,391
Thursday. Max asks you to look at his credit card statement with your superpower. Big mistake.

46
00:04:21,711 --> 00:04:26,448
He owes five thousand dollars and pays the minimum. The superpower doesn't show the balance. It shows the interest.

47
00:04:26,768 --> 00:04:34,141
At today's average card rates, paying the minimum on that balance could cost him over seven thousand dollars in interest. That's over two hundred hours. Just interest.

48
00:04:34,461 --> 00:04:40,138
Five full work weeks, for the privilege of having bought things earlier.

49
00:04:40,458 --> 00:04:43,778
Max closes the app, opens it again, and sets up a fixed payment. Character development.

50
00:04:44,578 --> 00:04:47,922
By Friday, the superpower is changing how you live.

51
00:04:48,242 --> 00:04:54,848
A concert ticket: two and a half hours. Totally worth it. You go.

52
00:04:55,168 --> 00:05:00,416
A random gadget at two in the morning: four hours. Not worth it. Cart deleted.

53
00:05:00,736 --> 00:05:07,284
You're not spending less on everything. You're spending more on the things that matter, and nothing on the things that don't.

54
00:05:07,604 --> 00:05:10,889
That's the real superpower. Not being cheap. Being clear.

55
00:05:11,689 --> 00:05:14,453
On Saturday, you visit Grandma. She asks why you keep squinting at the price of her biscuits.

56
00:05:14,773 --> 00:05:20,496
You explain the superpower. She laughs, and says she's been doing this her whole life. She just never called it a superpower.

57
00:05:20,816 --> 00:05:25,275
When I was young, she says, we asked one question before we bought anything. How long did I work for this?

58
00:05:25,595 --> 00:05:33,188
Then she hands you a biscuit. Zero hours. Free. The best price in the world.

59
00:05:33,988 --> 00:05:38,028
Now here's an uncomfortable twist. Is your hour worth more than it used to be?

60
00:05:38,348 --> 00:05:44,803
In March two thousand six, the average US hourly pay was about twenty dollars. Today it's thirty eight. Almost double.

61
00:05:45,123 --> 00:05:46,748
But prices rose too. The consumer price index went up about sixty nine percent over the same years.

62
00:05:47,068 --> 00:05:51,318
So, after inflation, an hour of work buys only about twelve percent more than it did in two thousand six.

63
00:05:51,638 --> 00:05:54,470
Twenty years of work history, and your hour got about twelve percent stronger. That's why every hour you spend matters.

64
00:05:55,270 --> 00:06:03,513
So what if you could use the superpower in reverse? Not to spend hours, but to buy them back?

65
00:06:03,833 --> 00:06:08,617
Here's the idea. Take just one hour of pay per week, thirty eight dollars, and invest it instead of spending it.

66
00:06:08,937 --> 00:06:16,077
That's about one hundred and sixty five dollars a month. Let's put it in an S and P five hundred index fund, starting in January twenty ten, at real prices.

67
00:06:16,397 --> 00:06:21,947
By September twenty twenty six, you've put in about thirty three thousand dollars.

68
00:06:22,267 --> 00:06:25,192
And it's worth about one hundred and seven thousand.

69
00:06:25,992 --> 00:06:29,185
Now turn that back into hours. One hundred and seven thousand dollars, at thirty eight dollars an hour, is over two thousand eight hundred hours.

70
00:06:29,505 --> 00:06:35,937
That is about one year and four months of full time work. Bought back with one hour a week.

71
00:06:36,257 --> 00:06:41,690
That money could be a career break, a safety net, a house deposit, or simply the freedom to say no to a job you hate.

72
00:06:42,010 --> 00:06:48,419
That's what money really is. Stored time. You decide whether it's stored for someone else, or for you.

73
00:06:49,219 --> 00:06:51,901
You don't need a superpower to do this. Three simple habits.

74
00:06:52,221 --> 00:06:57,980
One. Before any purchase over fifty dollars, divide the price by your take home hourly pay. Say the hours out loud.

75
00:06:58,300 --> 00:07:02,479
Two. Look at the big three first: housing, transport and food. That's where most of your hours go.

76
00:07:02,799 --> 00:07:09,533
Three. Pay yourself first. Move one hour of pay a week to savings or investing on payday, automatically.

77
00:07:09,853 --> 00:07:17,922
And one bonus habit. Once a year, add up your recurring payments and convert them into hours. It's the most honest number you'll see all year.

78
00:07:18,722 --> 00:07:22,611
The honest part. This is not financial advice.

79
00:07:22,931 --> 00:07:29,166
The prices in this story are examples, your hourly pay is your own, and taxes change everything.

80
00:07:29,486 --> 00:07:31,274
And the investing numbers use real past prices, which don't promise the future.

81
00:07:32,074 --> 00:07:36,137
Every price is a question. How many hours of my life is this worth?

82
00:07:36,457 --> 00:07:43,017
So tell me in the comments: what's one thing you buy that suddenly feels too expensive when you count it in hours?

83
00:07:43,337 --> 00:07:45,055
And I'll see you in the other timeline.

